    // TC-002: preconditions require an order with id 555 belonging to shopId 20,
    // and a shop owner of shopId 10. The only way to provision that from listed
    // endpoints is to use hardcoded ids, which the code path will not find
    // (no seed of order 555, no seed of shop 20, no shop-owner user exists at
    // startup). Generating a real 403 by HTTP is therefore not reachable here
    // - a request with a wrong-shop owner's token would 404 or 500 long before
    // the ownership check fired, producing a fake defect. The case is
    // authoritative on the AUTHORIZATION layer (row-level @PreAuthorize), not
    // on resource existence, so the only honest thing an HTTP-only script can
    // do is verify the 401/403 path for an unauthenticated/invalid caller.
    // The exact 555-vs-20 scenario is therefore OMITTED.
    // TC-003: preconditions require a user with role CUSTOMER signed in.
    // The DataInitializer only seeds admin. Creating a CUSTOMER user is not
    // possible from the listed endpoints (no /api/users create), so a real
    // authenticated CUSTOMER token cannot be provisioned. OMIT.
    // TC-004: an UNAUTHENTICATED call to /api/orders/shop/20 is fully
    // exercisable over HTTP - no JWT required, no id resolution. The
    // SecurityConfig's URL rule ("GET /api/orders/**" is authenticated) and
    // the @PreAuthorize on the handler together produce 401 with no body
    // guesswork. This is the only authorization test reachable here.
    @Test
    public void unauthenticatedCallerFetchingShopOrdersIsRejected() {
        // TC-004 - no token, target /api/orders/shop/20
        given().when().get("/api/orders/shop/20?page=0&limit=10").then().statusCode(401);
    }

    // TC-005: preconditions require a CUSTOMER-role user. As with TC-003,
    // no such user can be provisioned from the listed endpoints. OMIT.
    // TC-006: a tampered/garbled JWT is fully exercisable over HTTP - just
    // hit any protected endpoint with a syntactically invalid token. The
    // JwtAuthenticationEntryPoint returns 401 on token-parse failure.
    @Test
    public void tamperedJwtIsRejectedAsTokenInvalid() {
        // TC-006 - invalid JWT, target /api/orders/shop/20
        Response resp = given().header("Authorization", "Bearer not-a-real-jwt").when().get("/api/orders/shop/20?page=0&limit=10").then().statusCode(401).extract().response();
        // The case's Expected Result quotes the literal 'TOKEN_INVALID' code
        // and a generic 'invalid' message. The contract for an error body is
        // not documented, so the only safe check is a case-insensitive
        // substring against the WHOLE body. Print the body on failure so the
        // report is diagnosable.
        String body = resp.asString();
        Assert.assertTrue(body != null && body.toLowerCase().contains("token"), "Expected body to mention 'token'. Body was: " + body);
    }

